Clouds contain a genuine aerial microbiome—mostly microscopic life swept up from plants, soil, oceans, lakes, dust, and cities.

Common inhabitants include:

* Bacteria: Pseudomonas, Sphingomonas, Methylobacterium, Rhizobium, Janthinobacterium, Arthrobacter, and Lysinibacillus.
* Fungi and yeasts: Cladosporium, Penicillium, Trametes, Cryptococcus, Rhodotorula, and Sporobolomyces.
* Photosynthetic organisms: cyanobacteria and small green algae have been recovered and cultured from cloud water.
* Protist-related eukaryotes: including stramenopiles and alveolates.
* Viruses: especially viruses carried with or infecting airborne bacteria—though whether viruses count as “living organisms” remains definitional.

They aren’t merely dead debris. Researchers analyzing RNA in cloud droplets found metabolically active communities, including bacteria adapted to cold, ultraviolet radiation, scarce nutrients, acidity, and repeated drying and rehydration. One French study measured roughly 10^3–10^4 bacterial and archaeal cells and 10^2–10^3 eukaryotic cells per milliliter of cloud water. PLOS One study⁠￼

The wildest part is that some may help engineer their environment. Certain Pseudomonas and other bacteria possess surfaces that organize water into ice crystals at relatively warm subzero temperatures. That can potentially help form precipitation, returning the microbes to plants and soil. A Virginia study found ice-nucleating members of several bacterial families in rain and snow. ISME Journal study⁠￼

So clouds appear to be less like permanent floating continents and more like temporary microbial oases and transportation vessels:

surface ecosystem → aerosolization → cloud droplet → survival/activity → rain or snow → new surface ecosystem

Some microbes may not merely survive that water cycle—they may participate in closing it.